6. Full Philosophical Argument Specification
6.1 Philosophical Argument Schema
The philosophical argument schema defines the structure of a philosophical argument deposit.

It has the following fields:

argument_id. A string. Required.

argument_name. A string. Required.

argument_description. A string. Required.

problem_type. An array of strings. Required.

historical_period. An enum. Required. The values are ancient, medieval, modern, contemporary, other.

philosopher. An array of philosopher objects. Required.

conclusion. An object. Required. It contains statement and confidence.

premises. An array of premise objects. Required.

assumptions. An array of assumption objects. Optional.

objections. An array of objection objects. Optional.

responses. An array of response objects. Optional.

argument_form. An enum. Required. The values are deductive, inductive, abductive, transcendental, dialectical, reductio, other.

validity. An enum. Required. The values are valid, invalid, not_assessed.

soundness. An enum. Required. The values are sound, unsound, not_assessed.

argument_strength. An enum. Required. The values are strong, moderate, weak, uncertain.

key_concepts. An array of strings. Optional.

key_terms. An array of term objects. Optional.

related_arguments. An array of argument relations. Optional.

provenance. An object. Required.

work_reference. A string. Optional.

student_argument_reference. A string. Optional.

keywords. An array of strings. Optional.

notes. A string. Optional.

moderation_status. An enum. The values are pending, approved, rejected.

version. An integer.

supersedes. A string. Optional.

superseded_by. A string. Optional.

Each philosopher object has:

philosopher_id. A string.

philosopher_name. A string.

philosopher_role. An enum. The values are author, defender, critic, commentator, other.

philosopher_period. An enum. The values are ancient, medieval, modern, contemporary, other.

philosopher_school. A string. Optional.

Each premise object has:

premise_id. A string.

statement. A string.

premise_type. An enum. The values are conceptual, empirical, normative, methodological, logical, other.

support. An enum. The values are direct, indirect, background.

notes. A string. Optional.

Each assumption object has:

assumption_id. A string.

statement. A string.

assumption_type. An enum. The values are conceptual, empirical, normative, methodological, logical, other.

notes. A string. Optional.

Each objection object has:

objection_id. A string.

statement. A string.

target. An enum. The values are conclusion, premise, assumption, other.

target_id. A string. Optional.

objection_type. An enum. The values are rebuttal, undercutting, undermining, counterexample, dilemma, reductio, other.

strength. An enum. The values are strong, moderate, weak.

philosopher_id. A string. Optional.

source_reference. A string. Optional.

notes. A string. Optional.

Each response object has:

response_id. A string.

statement. A string.

target_objection_id. A string.

response_type. An enum. The values are denial, distinction, counterexample, concession, retorsion, transcendental_argument, other.

strength. An enum. The values are strong, moderate, weak.

philosopher_id. A string. Optional.

source_reference. A string. Optional.

notes. A string. Optional.

Each term object has:

term_id. A string.

term_name. A string.

term_definition. A string.

term_type. An enum. The values are technical, ordinary_language, historical, stipulative, other.

Each argument relation has:

relation_id. A string.

related_argument_id. A string.

relation_type. An enum. The values are supports, attacks, refines, generalizes, special_case, parallel, related, independent.

relation_note. A string. Optional.

